{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ConcurrencyAndCancellation","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"code":"@MainActor","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and built under Swift 6 strict concurrency. Every "},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"loadCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refreshCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"loadWithRetry","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"loadNextPage","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" call is "},{"code":"async","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", which raises three questions the moment a screen does more than one of them: what happens if a second call starts before the first finishes? How do you stop one early? And how do you force a store back to a known state outside of any of them? This article covers all three."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Overlapping-calls-only-ever-apply-the-latest-result","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overlapping calls only ever apply the latest result"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Nothing extra is needed to handle a screen firing off a second call before an earlier one has finished — a fast double-tap on Retry, or a new search query arriving before the previous one resolved:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 1) } \/\/ slow — still in flight","await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 2) } \/\/ fast — resolves first"]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Internally, every call stamps itself with a token before running "},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", and only applies its result — "},{"code":".data","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":".error","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", or the "},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" mapping "},{"code":"loadCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refreshCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" do — if that token is still the most recently started one by the time "},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" resolves. In the example above, article 1’s slower fetch resolving "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"after","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" article 2’s result is already showing never overwrites it; its result is silently dropped. "},{"code":"refresh(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refreshCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" follows the same rule: it only clears once the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"newest","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in-flight refresh finishes, not whichever overlapping one happens to resolve first. "},{"code":"loadNextPage(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"code":"isLoadingNextPage","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" follows it too."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"You don’t need to call "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" for this to hold — it’s automatic. Reach for "},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" when you additionally want to stop the older call’s work from running at all, not just prevent its result from landing."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Cancelling-explicitly","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Cancelling explicitly"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Call "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" to stop whatever call is currently running, without waiting for it to resolve:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["store.cancel() \/\/ e.g. from onDisappear, or right before firing off a newer query"]},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" (and "},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":") are left exactly as they were — cancelling never surfaces a "},{"code":"CancellationError","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" through "},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" the way letting "},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" itself fail would. This applies uniformly, including to "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/loadWithRetry(maxAttempts:backoff:_:)","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" cancelled mid-backoff: it stops retrying and leaves "},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" untouched, just like every other call. If nothing is in flight, "},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" does nothing."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Cancelling-automatically","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Cancelling automatically"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A store also cancels whatever it was still tracking when it’s deallocated, as a last-resort safety net — you don’t need to call "},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" from "},{"code":"deinit","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" yourself. In practice this rarely has anything to do: "},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/etc. already "},{"code":"await","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" their own work to completion before returning, so by the time a store can actually be deallocated, there’s normally nothing left in flight to cancel. It exists to guard against that assumption changing later, for free."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Resetting-outside-of-a-load","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Resetting outside of a load"},{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/reset(to:)","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" snaps a store back to "},{"code":".loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" (or any state you give it) without going through "},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" at all — useful for a full sign-out flow, where whatever the store was showing or fetching no longer applies to the next signed-in user:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["func signOut() {","    session.clear()","    articlesStore.reset() \/\/ back to .loading, as if freshly created","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Like "},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", it stops whatever operation is currently in flight; unlike "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","text":", it also immediately replaces "},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and clears "},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so a screen bound to the store reflects the reset right away instead of waiting for a cancelled operation to unwind."}]}]}],"kind":"article","schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"metadata":{"title":"Concurrency & Cancellation","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The guarantees "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" makes about overlapping, cancelled, and reset calls."}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Related Documentation","anchor":"Related-Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"]},{"title":"Modeling state","anchor":"Modeling-state","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatePreviewError","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Pagination"],"generated":true}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/concurrencyandcancellation"]}],"sections":[],"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateStore":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ScreenStateStore","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatestore","kind":"symbol","title":"ScreenStateStore","abstract":[{"text":"An ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" holder of a screen’s ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.